// The rightmost column of a 2 x n domino board splits the tilings in two: one upright
// domino leaves a 2 x (n-1) board, two flat dominoes leave a 2 x (n-2) board.
size(480,240);
pen edge = black + 1;
pen accent = rgb(0.10,0.30,0.60) + 2;
real y0 = 70, dy = 55;
label("stops after one column", (-90, y0 + 2*dy + 12), N);
label("stops after two columns", (180, y0 + 2*dy + 12), N);
draw((-180,y0)--(0,y0)--(0,y0+2*dy)--(-180,y0+2*dy)--cycle, edge);
draw((-180,y0+dy)--(0,y0+dy), edge);
draw((-135,y0)--(-135,y0+2*dy), edge);
draw((-90,y0)--(-90,y0+2*dy), edge);
draw((-45,y0)--(-45,y0+2*dy), edge);
draw((-45,y0)--(-45,y0+dy), accent);
label("$a_{n-1}$", (-90, y0 - 14), S);
draw((90,y0)--(270,y0)--(270,y0+2*dy)--(90,y0+2*dy)--cycle, edge);
draw((90,y0+dy)--(270,y0+dy), edge);
draw((135,y0)--(135,y0+2*dy), edge);
draw((180,y0)--(180,y0+2*dy), edge);
draw((225,y0)--(225,y0+2*dy), edge);
draw((180,y0)--(225,y0), accent);
draw((180,y0+dy)--(225,y0+dy), accent);
label("$a_{n-2}$", (180, y0 - 14), S);

solutionLatex:
Track the parity of the number of $1$s together with the final symbol. Write $E_0, E_1, O_0, O_1$ for the counts of strings with even or odd parity of $1$s that end in $0$ or $1$. Appending a $1$ flips the parity and may follow anything; appending a $0$ keeps the parity but may not follow a $0$. Hence

$E_0(n) = E_1(n-1)$, $E_1(n) = O_0(n-1) + O_1(n-1)$, $O_0(n) = O_1(n-1)$, $O_1(n) = E_0(n-1) + E_1(n-1)$,

starting from length $1$, where the strings $0$ and $1$ give $E_0 = 1$, $E_1 = 0$, $O_0 = 0$, $O_1 = 1$.

The table through length $10$ is

$n=2$: $0, 1, 1, 1$;
$n=3$: $1, 2, 1, 1$;
$n=4$: $2, 2, 1, 3$;
$n=5$: $2, 4, 3, 4$;
$n=6$: $4, 7, 4, 6$;
$n=7$: $7, 10, 6, 11$;
$n=8$: $10, 17, 11, 17$;
$n=9$: $17, 28, 17, 27$;
$n=10$: $28, 44, 27, 45$.

Only the two even-parity entries matter at length $10$, so the answer is $E_0(10) + E_1(10) = 28 + 44 = 72$.
